  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 134 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging is pushed eastward next week thanks to troughing \nto the north and west. As a result, while temperatures will remain \nabove normal for mid-September, they will at least tick down \nthroughout the week. Highs in the mid-to-upper 90s will be \ncommonplace during the first half of the week, with temperatures \njust over 100 degrees in/near the river valleys. By Thursday and \nFriday, low-to-mid 90s become more prevalent across the area. \nMeanwhile, surface troughing and pulses in the flow aloft yield \nincreasing rain chances daily across the western half of the area, \nespecially in/around the higher terrain south and west of the Pecos \nRiver. Chances for these locations generally range from 20-50%. \nGoing into next weekend, model spread and uncertainty begins to \nincrease. The NBM at least suggests temperatures will continue to \ncool as ridging aloft is dampened by passing shortwaves. However, \nthe extent of cooling remains uncertain. It also remains to be seen \nif these passing shortwaves will bring better rain chances to a \nlarger portion of the area, or even try to push a front our \ndirection. Details will be refined as model consistency increases, \nso stay tuned for updates!\n\nSprang",
